    "slug": "bhagavad-gita-on-death-soul-after-death",
    "oldUrl": "https://sciencedivine.org/blog/bhagavad-gita-on-death-soul-after-death",
    "title": "Bhagavad Gita on Death: What Happens to Soul After Death?",
    "excerpt": "What happens to the soul when the body dies? Explore the Bhagavad Gita’s teachings on death, the eternal Atman, karma, rebirth, the final state of mind and liberation from the cycle of birth and death.",
    "category": "Festivals & Traditions",
    "categorySlug": "festivals-traditions",
    "date": "Sep 3, 2026",
    "datePublished": "2026-09-03T08:01:57.347+00:00",
    "readTime": "2 min read",
    "words": 391,
    "author": {
      "name": "Sakshi Shree",
      "role": "Enlightened Master & Founder, Science Divine",
      "avatar": "https://sciencedivine.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/03/Sakshi-Shree-Profile-1.webp"
    },
    "image": "/blog/images/bhagavad-gita-on-death-soul-after-death.webp",
    "content": "Death is one of humanity’s deepest mysteries. What happens when the body stops functioning? Does our existence end completely, or does something continue? And if the soul survives, where does it go? Thousands of years ago, the Bhagavad Gita addressed these very questions through the dialogue between Lord Krishna and Arjuna on the battlefield of Kurukshetra. Arjuna was overwhelmed by grief at the thought of the deaths that the coming battle would bring. Krishna responded by shifting his understanding from the temporary physical body to the eternal Atman, the true Self or soul . According to the Bhagavad Gita on death , the destruction of the physical body is not the destruction of the soul. Krishna describes the Atman as unborn, eternal and unaffected by bodily death in Bhagavad Gita 2.20. Understanding this distinction between body and Self is essential to understanding what happens to the soul after death according to the Bhagavad Gita . What Does the Bhagavad Gita Say About Death? Th...",
    "contentHtml": "<p>Death is one of humanity’s deepest mysteries. What happens when the body stops functioning? Does our existence end completely, or does something continue? And if the soul survives, where does it go?</p><p>Thousands of years ago, the <strong>Bhagavad Gita addressed these very questions</strong> through the dialogue between Lord Krishna and Arjuna on the battlefield of Kurukshetra.</p><p>Arjuna was overwhelmed by grief at the thought of the deaths that the coming battle would bring. Krishna responded by shifting his understanding from the temporary physical body to the eternal <strong>Atman, the true Self or soul</strong>.</p><p>According to the <strong>Bhagavad Gita on death</strong>, the destruction of the physical body is not the destruction of the soul. Krishna describes the Atman as unborn, eternal and unaffected by bodily death in Bhagavad Gita 2.20.</p><p>Understanding this distinction between body and Self is essential to understanding <strong>what happens to the soul after death according to the Bhagavad Gita</strong>.<br/><img alt=\"Bhagavad Gita on Death – spiritual image featuring the Bhagavad Gita, a glowing diya and sunset, illustrating what happens to the soul after death.\" fetchPriority=\"high\" width=\"1672\" height=\"941\" decoding=\"async\" data-nimg=\"1\" class=\"rounded-lg md:mx-auto lg:mx-auto  xl:mx-auto\" style=\"color:transparent\" sizes=\"100vw\" srcSet=\"https://cdn.typeflo.io/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fauth.typeflo.io%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Fposts%2Fpublic%2F6eae6931-f95c-4bcd-b740-e9c3acce8146%2F1147ca78-b107-4950-a7b8-e16174ba3adc%2Fbxxqpre.webp%3F1788430135663&amp;w=640&amp;q=75 640w, https://cdn.typeflo.io/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fauth.typeflo.io%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Fposts%2Fpublic%2F6eae6931-f95c-4bcd-b740-e9c3acce8146%2F1147ca78-b107-4950-a7b8-e16174ba3adc%2Fbxxqpre.webp%3F1788430135663&amp;w=750&amp;q=75 750w, https://cdn.typeflo.io/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fauth.typeflo.io%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Fposts%2Fpublic%2F6eae6931-f95c-4bcd-b740-e9c3acce8146%2F1147ca78-b107-4950-a7b8-e16174ba3adc%2Fbxxqpre.webp%3F1788430135663&amp;w=828&amp;q=75 828w, https://cdn.typeflo.io/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fauth.typeflo.io%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Fposts%2Fpublic%2F6eae6931-f95c-4bcd-b740-e9c3acce8146%2F1147ca78-b107-4950-a7b8-e16174ba3adc%2Fbxxqpre.webp%3F1788430135663&amp;w=1080&amp;q=75 1080w, https://cdn.typeflo.io/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fauth.typeflo.io%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Fposts%2Fpublic%2F6eae6931-f95c-4bcd-b740-e9c3acce8146%2F1147ca78-b107-4950-a7b8-e16174ba3adc%2Fbxxqpre.webp%3F1788430135663&amp;w=1200&amp;q=75 1200w, https://cdn.typeflo.io/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fauth.typeflo.io%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Fposts%2Fpublic%2F6eae6931-f95c-4bcd-b740-e9c3acce8146%2F1147ca78-b107-4950-a7b8-e16174ba3adc%2Fbxxqpre.webp%3F1788430135663&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75 1920w, https://cdn.typeflo.io/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fauth.typeflo.io%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Fposts%2Fpublic%2F6eae6931-f95c-4bcd-b740-e9c3acce8146%2F1147ca78-b107-4950-a7b8-e16174ba3adc%2Fbxxqpre.webp%3F1788430135663&amp;w=2048&amp;q=75 2048w, https://cdn.typeflo.io/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fauth.typeflo.io%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Fposts%2Fpublic%2F6eae6931-f95c-4bcd-b740-e9c3acce8146%2F1147ca78-b107-4950-a7b8-e16174ba3adc%2Fbxxqpre.webp%3F1788430135663&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75 3840w\" src=\"https://cdn.typeflo.io/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fauth.typeflo.io%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Fposts%2Fpublic%2F6eae6931-f95c-4bcd-b740-e9c3acce8146%2F1147ca78-b107-4950-a7b8-e16174ba3adc%2Fbxxqpre.webp%3F1788430135663&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75\"/></p><h2 id=\"what-does-the-bhagavad-gita-say-about-death\">What Does the Bhagavad Gita Say About Death?</h2><p>The Bhagavad Gita does not present death as the absolute end of existence. It presents death primarily as a transition involving the embodied soul.</p><p>The physical body is temporary. It is born, grows, changes, ages and eventually dies. The Atman, however, is described differently.</p><p>In <strong>Bhagavad Gita 2.20</strong>, Krishna explains that the soul does not come into existence at birth and does not cease to exist when the body dies. The transformations that affect the body do not destroy the eternal Self.</p><p>This is one of the most important <strong>Bhagavad Gita teachings on death</strong>.</p><p>Our fear of death often comes from complete identification with the body. If we believe, “I am only this body,” the destruction of the body appears to be the destruction of ourselves.</p><p>Krishna asks Arjuna to look deeper.</p><p>The body changes. The observer of those changes, the Self, is different from those temporary forms.</p><p>From the Gita’s spiritual perspective, therefore, death is not simply about asking, “What ends?” It is also about asking, <strong>“What remains?”</strong><br/></p><div class=\"nc-SectionBecomeAnAuthor w-full rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 dark:bg-neutral-800 dark:border-neutral-700 px-5 py-4 \"><div class=\"custom-cta-section flex flex-col\"><h4 class=\"custom-cta-title font-semibold text-gray-900 dark:text-white text-[19px] leading-snug !m-0\">Design Your Destiny</h4><p class=\"custom-cta-subtitle text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400 !mt-1 !mb-0\">The Bhagavad Gita reminds us that spiritual transformation does not begin at the moment of death—it begins with how consciously we live today.  Join Design Your Destiny and begin exploring the inner patterns, attachments and beliefs shaping your life. Move from unconscious living toward greater awareness, clarity and inner transformation.</p><a href=\"https://www.udemy.com/course/inner-transformation-masterclass-with-sakshi-shree-hindi/?srsltid=AfmBOoogBhPRKvoviHagsu_eRQISN1B50noJmvEWU2CK-78Bl0MxZ-Yk\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" class=\"custom-cta-btn self-start inline-flex items-center px-4 h-9 rounded-lg bg-primary-500 text-white text-sm font-medium hover:bg-primary-600 transition-colors !no-underline mt-3\">Start Your Journey Today!</a>",
